When Pollo Campero opened on the Northwest Side 10 years ago, people waited in line for hours to taste the Guatemalan fried chicken. When Shake Shack opened last year in River North, lines stretched down Ohio Street. When Leghorn Chicken opened last year, lines went out the door. Chick-fil-A, ditto. You see a pattern? Restaurant opening. Chainlike food. Hype throttles to overdrive. Lines out the door.
And then? It always happens. The hype and the long lines never sustain. Wait a month, and you wonder what the big deal was in the first place.
This week, the fast-food hype machine baton got passed — in temporary possession, of course — to The Halal Guys, which comes to Chicago from Midtown Manhattan, where the Middle Eastern food cart of the same name at 53rd Street and Sixth Avenue has drawn long lines day and night, during all seasons and weather, for 25 years. This is as much about circumstance as cuisine: There��s the romanticism of street food, efficiently slopped onto an aluminum container, eaten hot and fast as you sit by the fountain statue outside the Calyon Building. The gyro rice plates are also served relatively quickly and beat anything at Panera Bread.
The Halal Guys in Chicago, at 49 W. Division St. in the Gold Coast, operates differently from the New York food cart format. The restaurant is a storefront wedged between a Wingstop and a Jimmy John��s, with a counter to order, flat-screen TV menus and seating for a dozen-plus inside. It��s a full-fledged bricks-and-mortar fast-food franchise now, from the same restaurant consulting firm that made Five Guys and Qdoba ubiquitous presences.
Chicago��s Halal Guys opened Saturday, and not surprisingly, the line was crazy. Management claimed that five times the number of customers as were expected actually showed up -- so many that the store had to close early due to dwindling supplies. I visited on its third day, and even though I arrived at 2 p.m. on a Monday, it was 45 minutes before I picked up my order.
Menu concepts don��t come simpler. You can order a rice platter ($7.99 small/$8.99 regular) or a pita sandwich ($6.99). The protein choices are chicken, gyro meat or falafel. There are also fries, hummus, tahini and, for dessert, baklava. I've just recited the entire menu.
What gives Halal Guys its belly-glowing mystique are the two accompanying sauces: White sauce is essentially mayonnaise diluted to a dressing, and the legendary hot sauce is legitimately spicy, as in fanning-your-tongue-in-futility spicy.
I mean this in the positive connotation, but the most apt flavor comparison is to Taco Bell if it upped its game. There is nothing so mind-blowingly delicious that requires a daily fix — I still think Naf Naf Grill, though not certified halal, remains the standard bearer of Middle Eastern quick-service in Chicago.
Yet, every component on a Halal Guys platter is engineered for high-sodium, high-fat, pleasure center arousal. Your brain can��t help but agree the food is worth another bite. The gyro meats are crusty, peppery knobs of lamb/beef hybrid, and even the chopped shards of chicken are deeply flavored. Combined onto a plate, it��s a gloppy frontal assault of meat, sauce, rice and heat. It��s like taking the contents of a burrito and squeezing it out of its tortilla tube. Who doesn't like that?
