Edwards did little campaigning and has only been able to raise a few hundred dollars so far for his campaign. Other than the most recent governor's race, Louisiana has also gone decidedly Republican in statewide elections in recent years.
Even the Louisiana Democratic Party has worries about Edwards as a candidate; it has declined to officially endorse him or work for his campaign, in part because he has failed to meet the state legal deadlines for his campaign finance paperwork over the past couple of months.
The four weeks before the general election elections are expected to essentially be a victory lap for Schroder, who would take over for interim Treasurer Ron Henson if he wins next month. Henson was appointed treasurer in early 2017 after John Kennedy stepped down to join the U.S. Senate.
Still, Schroder said he is not taking the runoff election for granted. He expects Democratic turnout on Nov. 18 to be high because of runoff elections for mayor and city council in New Orleans. Few major elections will be happening elsewhere in the more Republican parts of the state on that day. That seems to make Schroder nervous.
